## Why This Is Asked

Tests for high-concurrency, real-time messaging with ordering and durability are common in collaboration apps. This question probes ability to design end-to-end tests that cover connection scalability, in-order delivery, offline replay, backpressure, and resilience to broker outages.

## Key Concepts

- WebSocket load testing and client choreography
- In-order delivery guarantees with per-room sequencing
- Durable stores for replay (Redis Streams, Kafka, or similar)
- Backpressure handling and reconnection logic
- Failure modes: partial broker outages, network partitions

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test sketch for sequencing validation
let roomId = 'room-42';
let lastSeq = 0;
ws.onmessage = (evt) => {
  const msg = JSON.parse(evt.data);
  if (msg.roomId === roomId) {
    if (msg.seq <= lastSeq) fail('out-of-order');
    lastSeq = msg.seq;
  }
};
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure end-to-end latency under peak load and under broker outage?
- What tooling would you use to generate 10k concurrent connections and validate ordering?

## Why This Is Asked

Evaluates practical API versioning and deprecation testing strategies, assessing the candidate's ability to design comprehensive test coverage for versioned APIs with concrete expectations and appropriate tooling recommendations.

## Key Concepts

- Version routing via HTTP headers
- Backward compatibility validation between API versions
- Deprecation signaling and migration path implementation
- Negative testing and edge case handling

## Code Example

```javascript
describe('GET /users/{id} versioned endpoints', () => {
  it('defaults to v1 when Version header is missing', async () => {
    const res = await request.get('/users/123')
    expect(res.body).toHaveProperty('username')
    expect(res.body).toHaveProperty('email')
    expect(res.body).not.toHaveProperty('phone')
  })

  it('returns v2 format with include_email=true', async () => {
    const res = await request
      .get('/users/123?include_email=true')
      .set('X-API-Version', 'v2')
    expect(res.body).toEqual({
      username: expect.any(String),
      email: expect.any(String),
      phone: expect.any(String),
      address: expect.any(Object)
    })
  })
})

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for idempotent POSTs prevent duplicate orders when clients retry. It shows understanding of race conditions, state management, and how to validate that retries are safe across failure modes.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotency keys
- Duplicate detection and database invariants
- Retry semantics and safe side effects
- Observability and tooling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example idempotent POST test (pseudo)
async function testIdempotentPost() {
  const key = 'abcd-1234';
  const payload = { customerId: 'c1', items: [{sku:'A', qty:1}] };
  const r1 = await fetch('/orders', { method:'POST', headers:{'Idempotency-Key': key}, body: JSON.stringify(payload) });
  const r2 = await fetch('/orders', { method:'POST', headers:{'Idempotency-Key': key}, body: JSON.stringify(payload) });
  // asserts: r1.status==201, r2.status==200 and r1.json().orderId===r2.json().orderId
}
```

